{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Assemble the people, etc., that they may hear, and that they may learn, etc.</b> One must know why he repeated the word למען twice, and it was not sufficient to say ״למען ישמעו וילמדו״ — as indeed He said in the verse after this one, where He said ״ישמעו ולמדו״?" "<b>And it appears</b> that since the verse said ״Assemble the people, the men and the women and the little ones״ to read the book of the Torah in their ears, and it has already preceded us that the women are not obligated in Torah study, as it is written (Deuteronomy 11:19) ״ולמדתם אתם את בניכם״, and our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Kiddushin 29b) — and not your daughters:" "<b>Therefore</b> the verse divided two levels in this commandment, and said first a division that is equal for the men and for the women, which is ״that they may hear״ — meaning, that they may hear the commandments of the Torah that they are obligated to perform, and in this matter they are equal, for the women too are obligated in negative commandments and in positive commandments that are not time-bound." "<b>And afterward</b> He said a separate division pertaining to the men alone, which is ״and that they may learn״, which is Torah study; and therefore He did not say ״that they may hear and learn״, for then it would have been understood that both are under one category." "<b>Furthermore</b>, I saw the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Chagigah 3a), who said, and this is their language: ״The women to hear, and the men to learn,״ end quote. And these are our very words, and they were clever to put the women before the men in the same order that the verse set forth: ״that they may hear״, and afterward ״and that they may learn״ — just as we have written."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:51.612858419Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}